// =============================================================================
//  probe_melee.hml — acceptance probe for src/sim/melee.hml + the melee half
//                    of src/sim/weapons.hml.        DIRECTION.md §10.
// =============================================================================
//
//  Run it FROM THE NIGHTSHADE REPO ROOT, and BOTH ways — the golden hashes are
//  what make running it twice mean anything:
//
//      hemlockc -O1 tools/probe_melee.hml -o /tmp/pmel && /tmp/pmel
//      hemlock  tools/probe_melee.hml
//
//  It exits 1 on any failure AND on an assertion-COUNT mismatch.  A probe that
//  silently stops running half its checks is worse than no probe.
//
//  WHAT IT IS TRYING TO PROVE, in the order the design cares about:
//
//    A  the TABLE is the design — the role orderings, against LITERALS
//    B  TTK against ai.hml's real HP column, against LITERALS
//    C  a sweep cannot pass through the smallest body in the game
//    D  the swing state machine: one strike per swing, buffered input, ticks
//    E  hit registration is combat.hml's, not a second copy of it
//    F  cleave, dedupe and the nearest-first cap
//    G  reach is the spear's identity and it is measurable
//    H  heads, plates and flanks behave exactly as they do for a bullet
//    I  terrain occludes a swing
//    J  NO DURABILITY — blow 1 and blow 10 000 are the same blow
//    K  melee consumes ZERO randomness
//    L  stagger: magnitude order, max-not-sum, decay, and not on a corpse
//    M  the GAME_DESIGN §2.5 five-signal contract, on every swing shape
//    N  determinism: same seed identical, different seed different,
//       compiled == interpreted
//    O  cost, with the loadavg printed next to it
// =============================================================================

